TOPICS TO COVER (LangGraph 1.x, plain Python, no real API keys; use FakeListChatModel for model nodes)
S1 State (TypedDict), nodes return a partial-update dict, add_node, add_edge, START, END, compile, invoke. Unknown keys are silently dropped. A node must return a dict.
S2 Router (conditional edges): a function that returns a node NAME as a quoted string; router is not a node (no add_node); add_conditional_edges(source, router).
S3 Loops: router sometimes returns a node that already ran; use state.get("x", 0) for slots that may be unset; KeyError if not.
S4 Stopping rules: success exit first, then attempts limit (attempts >= N), then loop; use >= not ==; recursion limit is a crash, a count exit is a designed decision.
S5 Reducers: Annotated[list[str], operator.add]; with a reducer return the CHANGE (a list), without a reducer return the new value; operator.add on text glues strings.
S6 Tools and failures: a tool is a plain function called inside a node (not added to the graph); raise vs return decides behavior (the returned text is ignored if not stored); try/except with specific error types (ConnectionError, TimeoutError, KeyError, ValueError...); a failed call is not a result but IS an attempt; external dictionary counters persist across calls (a second call continues, it does not restart); RetryPolicy vs try/except + router.
S7 Model nodes: model.invoke(prompt).text; prompt as f-string from state (single quotes inside braces); model output is untrusted: strip(), lower(), "x in allowed_list", default to "escalate"; a model call can time out (try/except TimeoutError); LLM advises, code decides (never let the model approve on its own; guard with hard conditions such as documents >= 3); prompt injection; truthy trap (if state.get("urgency"): fires for ANY non-empty text, use == "escalate"); a value written into state changes nothing unless a router reads it; human-escalation rule goes FIRST in the router; reset external dictionaries (counters, down flags) before every test.

FACTS TO KEEP CONSISTENT (my own project, use as reference)
- archive counter: calls += 1, then raise ConnectionError if calls <= fail_first. With fail_first = 2, calls 1 and 2 fail, call 3 succeeds. calls persists between node visits.
- gather_docs: count = attempts + 1 always; on success documents = old documents + 1; on failure documents unchanged, error set.
- router order: criteria NOT FOUND -> escalate; documents >= 3 -> ready; attempts >= 5 -> escalate; else gather_docs.
- fetch_policy -> gather_docs is a fixed edge, so even an escalated case runs gather_docs once.

